    "id": "train-3650",
    "question": "Martin went to a store to buy a new television set. He decided to spend no more than $1,000 and the sales clerk offered him the TV for $100 less and an additional 20% off. How much lower was the price of the television than the amount Martin decided to spend?",
    "gold": "280",
    "answer": "The seller has offered him the TV for 1000 - 100 = $<<1000-100=900>>900.\nThe discount on the television was 900 * 20/100 = $<<900*20/100=180>>180.\nThe final price of the television is therefore 900 - 180 = $<<900-180=720>>720.\nMartin was able to save 1000 - 720 = $<<1000-720=280>>280.\n#### 280"
  },
  "generation": {
    "text": "To determine how much lower the price of the television is compared to the amount Martin decided to spend, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Identify the maximum amount Martin is willing to spend, which is $1,000.\n2. Calculate the price of the television after the $100 discount and the additional 20% off.\n3. Find the difference between the amount Martin decided to spend and the final price of the television.\n\nLet's start with the first step. The maximum amount Martin is willing to spend is $1,000.\n\nNext, we calculate the price of the television after the $100 discount. The price after the $100 discount is:\n\\[ 1000 - 100 = 900 \\]\n\nNow, we apply the additional 20% off to the $900 price. A 20% discount means Martin pays 80% of the $900 price. So, we calculate 80% of $900:\n\\[ 900 \\times 0.80 = 720 \\]\n\nThe final price of the television is $720. To find out how much lower this price is compared to the amount Martin decided to spend, we subtract the final price from the amount Martin decided to spend:\n\\[ 1000 - 720 = 280 \\]\n\nTherefore, the price of the television is \\(\\boxed{280}\\) dollars lower than the amount Martin decided to spend.",
